{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":1,"cacheRead":28998,"cacheWrite":757,"totalTokens":29758,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":5e-7,"cacheRead":0.00028998,"cacheWrite":0.000094625,"total":0.000385305,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"text_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"`double_underline` now works as a first-class style. The test suite shows the same 12 failures before and after my change (all in `test_syntax.py` and similar), so there are no regressions.\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`rich/style.py`**\n  - `Style.__init__` accepts `double_underline`, and `Style.double_underline` is a boolean property.\n  - `double_underline` is an alias for the existing `underline2` bit (ANSI SGR 21), so `+`, `combine()`, `copy()` and the rest of the style machinery preserve it without extra code.\n  - The parser accepts `double_underline`, `double-underline` and `du`.\n  - `get_html_style()` emits `text-decoration: underline double`.\n- **`rich/console.py`**: `export_svg()` emits `text-decoration: underline double;`.\n\nChecked behaviour:\n\n- `Style(double_underline=True)` no longer raises `TypeError`.\n- Markup `[double_underline]Sample[/]` and `[double-underline red]Alert[/]` render with `\\x1b[21m`.\n- The SVG export contains the double-underline CSS rule.\n\nI also added tests to `tests/test_style.py` covering the aliases, the constructor, `+`/`combine()`, and the HTML output. They pass.\n\nOne behaviour to be aware of: because the bit is shared, `str(Style.parse(\"du\"))` returns `underline2`, not `double_underline`. The two names are interchangeable, so I didn't change the canonical string form."}}
